# Caddy TLS terminator for the ALCF Agent dashboard.
#
# Why: the dashboard's chat is an xterm terminal that uses the browser Clipboard
# API (navigator.clipboard.readText/writeText) for copy/paste. That API is gated
# to "secure contexts". Plain http://<host>:8787 is NOT a secure context (except
# the localhost special-case, which is fragile across hostnames/proxies), so
# Chrome blocks paste. Serving the dashboard over HTTPS makes it a secure
# context everywhere and copy/paste works.
#
# Caddy auto-generates a local self-signed cert (the browser shows a one-time
# "not private" warning you click through — expected for a local self-signed
# cert). It listens on the public dashboard port and reverse-proxies to the
# dashboard running on loopback inside the container.
#
# ${ALCF_DASHBOARD_PORT} and ${ALCF_DASHBOARD_INTERNAL_PORT} are substituted by
# the entrypoint at start.

{
	# No email / no ACME — we only use Caddy's internal (self-signed) CA here.
	auto_https disable_redirects
	admin off
	# Serve a cert even when the client sends no/unknown SNI (e.g. hitting
	# the container by IP): fall back to the localhost cert.
	default_sni localhost
}

# Bind explicit local hostnames so Caddy's internal CA pre-issues certs for
# them (a bare ":port" site can fail the TLS handshake with 'internal error'
# because no cert matches the SNI/IP). localhost covers the documented
# https://localhost:PORT entry point; 127.0.0.1 covers IP access.
https://localhost:${ALCF_DASHBOARD_PORT}, https://127.0.0.1:${ALCF_DASHBOARD_PORT} {
	tls internal
	# WebSocket + streaming friendly; the dashboard chat uses a WS PTY.
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:${ALCF_DASHBOARD_INTERNAL_PORT}
}
